CORE
main.cpp
--cpp11 --unwind 5
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ the exception object is a copy: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ the rethrown exception is the same object: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ e and the exception object: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.4\] line \d+ each object destroyed exactly once: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.5\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^\*\* 1 of \d+ failed
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
--
Fixed: a handler parameter of reference type became one of the referenced
type, bound by a bitwise copy that the end of the handler destroyed, and
the exception object was never destroyed (with the rethrow, destroyed[1]
was 2).  The reference now binds to the exception object, and the exit of
the last active handler destroys it unless it is rethrown.
